  * kern.sweet_reboot_mode (string; "fastboot" is accepted for "bootloader").
  *
  * Panic: unchanged (no reset, the screen/log stays) unless the boot-arg is
- * sweetreboot=bootloader; then a panic also resets into fastboot (unattended
- * tests). The sysctl does not affect panics.
+ * sweetreboot=bootloader (a panic resets into fastboot) or sweetpanicreboot=1
+ * (patch 1020: a panic resets in normal mode, so ABL boots the boot partition
+ * again). The reset happens at kPEPanicDiagnosticsDone, which every panic
+ * reaches whatever the debug boot-arg says (debug=0xc sets panicDebugging,
+ * so kPEPanicRestartCPU never comes), after the panic text went to the
+ * console ring; sweet_ufs_flush_final writes the ring to the log area first
+ * (tools/sweet-log-pull.sh). The sysctl does not affect panics.
+ *
+ * Hardware watchdog (patch 1020): boot-arg sweetwdt=N (seconds, 5..120; 0/absent
+ * = off) arms the APSS WDT (live DT qcom,wdt@17980000 "qcom,msm-watchdog",
+ * 32768 Hz sleep clock; Linux drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c kpss offsets) with a
+ * bite after N s, and a kernel thread pets it every N/4 s. A wedged scheduler,
+ * or a panic that never reaches the reset above, stops the pets and the bite
+ * resets the SoC (bark interrupt left off: XNU has no handler for it). Halt
+ * disables it. Test: sysctl kern.sweet_reboot_test=1 panics, =2 stops the pets
+ * and spins this CPU with interrupts off (sweetreboot test-panic|test-hang).
  *
  * Reset time may be non-preemptible (halt_all_cpus, panic): everything is
  * mapped by sweet_reboot_init() and the SPMI helpers run lock-less there.
